  "solution": {
   "c": "#include <stdio.h>\n#include <string.h>\n\nint main(void) {\n    int q;\n    scanf(\"%d\", &q);\n    int stack[1000], mins[1000], top = -1;\n    for (int i = 0; i < q; i++) {\n        char op[16];\n        scanf(\"%15s\", op);\n        if (strcmp(op, \"push\") == 0) {\n            int x;\n            scanf(\"%d\", &x);\n            stack[++top] = x;\n            mins[top] = (top == 0 || x < mins[top - 1]) ? x : mins[top - 1];\n        } else if (strcmp(op, \"pop\") == 0) top--;\n        else if (strcmp(op, \"top\") == 0) printf(\"%d\\n\", stack[top]);\n        else printf(\"%d\\n\", mins[top]);\n    }\n    return 0;\n}\n",
   "cpp": "#include <algorithm>\n#include <iostream>\n#include <stack>\n#include <string>\nusing namespace std;\n\nint main() {\n    int q;\n    cin >> q;\n    stack<int> st, mins;\n    for (int i = 0; i < q; i++) {\n        string op;\n        cin >> op;\n        if (op == \"push\") {\n            int x;\n            cin >> x;\n            st.push(x);\n            mins.push(mins.empty() ? x : min(x, mins.top()));\n        } else if (op == \"pop\") { st.pop(); mins.pop(); }\n        else if (op == \"top\") cout << st.top() << \"\\n\";\n        else cout << mins.top() << \"\\n\";\n    }\n    return 0;\n}\n",
   "java": "import java.util.*;\n\nclass Main {\n    public static void main(String[] args) {\n        Scanner in = new Scanner(System.in);\n        int q = in.nextInt();\n        Deque<Integer> st = new ArrayDeque<>(), mins = new ArrayDeque<>();\n        StringBuilder out = new StringBuilder();\n        for (int i = 0; i < q; i++) {\n            String op = in.next();\n            if (op.equals(\"push\")) {\n                int x = in.nextInt();\n                st.push(x);\n                mins.push(mins.isEmpty() ? x : Math.min(x, mins.peek()));\n            } else if (op.equals(\"pop\")) { st.pop(); mins.pop(); }\n            else if (op.equals(\"top\")) out.append(st.peek()).append(\"\\n\");\n            else out.append(mins.peek()).append(\"\\n\");\n        }\n        System.out.print(out);\n    }\n}\n",
   "py": "import sys\n\ndata = sys.stdin.read().split()\nq = int(data[0])\npos = 1\nstack, mins = [], []\nfor _ in range(q):\n    op = data[pos]\n    pos += 1\n    if op == \"push\":\n        x = int(data[pos])\n        pos += 1\n        stack.append(x)\n        mins.append(x if not mins else min(x, mins[-1]))\n    elif op == \"pop\":\n        stack.pop()\n        mins.pop()\n    elif op == \"top\":\n        print(stack[-1])\n    else:\n        print(mins[-1])\n"
  },
  "explain": "Scanning for the minimum would cost O(n). Instead, store with every element **the minimum of everything below it and itself**. Keep a second stack of minimums (or store pairs): when pushing x, the new minimum is min(x, current minimum); when popping, discard both. Then `getMin` just reads the top of the minimum stack. Equal values are fine because each push records its own minimum.",
